  :adr/doc_type "adr"
  :adr/source-format "edn"
  :adr/date "2026-07-19"
  :adr/status :accepted
  :adr/title "kotoba-lang/watchlist-screen: real OFAC/UN sanctions data ingestion + tiered name matching"
  :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/watchlist-screen]
  :adr/related ["orgs/kotoba-lang/aml" "orgs/kotoba-lang/ekyc" "orgs/kotoba-lang/identity"
                "orgs/kotoba-lang/mrz"]
  :adr/context
  "Phase B of the eKYC/AML vendor plan approved earlier this session
   (following Phase A, kotoba-lang/mrz, ADR-2607198200). kotoba-lang/aml
   already exists (MATURITY R2 live transport) but its own README states
   plainly: 'This repo does not score, enforce, or fetch live lists.' Its
   two configured routes (:yabai -> a cyber threat-intel actor scoring
   domains/infrastructure, :malak -> an unactivated blockchain-address risk
   stub) are NOT sanctions databases -- confirmed by reading both adapters
   and the sibling actors' own source during this session's planning phase.
   No real sanctions/PEP/OFAC/SDN dataset existed anywhere in this
   workspace prior to this ADR (exhaustive grep of orgs/ and 90-docs/
   returned zero hits).

   Unlike document/biometric verification (ADR-2607198200's mrz.glyph
   half, explicitly scoped down because no real training/benchmark data
   exists), REAL, LEGITIMATE, PUBLICLY-LICENSED sanctions/PEP data sources
   actually exist and are freely downloadable: the OFAC SDN List
   (treasury.gov, public XML, no authentication) and the UN Security
   Council Consolidated List (scsanctions.un.org, public XML, no
   authentication). This ADR's parsers were built and tested against the
   ACTUAL LIVE FILES fetched during development (not reconstructed from
   memory) -- the live OFAC file (19,169 records, published 07/17/2026)
   and the live UN file (1,010 records, dateGenerated 2026-07-18) were both
   fetched, their real XML schemas read directly, and every field mapping
   verified against real data before any fixture or test was written.

   The EU Consolidated Financial Sanctions List was investigated the same
   way and found NOT freely fetchable: the EU's machine-readable source
   (the FSD/FSF API at webgate.ec.europa.eu) requires a registered access
   token -- every unauthenticated attempt during this session returned
   HTTP 500. Rather than guess at a schema never seen live (the exact
   false-confidence failure mode this whole eKYC/AML effort is structured
   to avoid), this ADR ships watchlist.adapters.eu-consolidated as an
   explicit, documented not-implemented stub."
  :adr/decision
  "Add kotoba-lang/watchlist-screen, a mostly-portable .cljc library:

   - watchlist.model -- entity/list-manifest records, match-tier vocabulary
     (:exact/:fuzzy-high/:fuzzy-low), staleness policy (pure, clock-as-param
     mirroring isekai.fork-stats' own convention for the same reason).
   - watchlist.match -- normalization (documented gap: strips diacritics as
     punctuation rather than transliterating) + a from-scratch Jaro-Winkler
     implementation, verified against Winkler's own published reference
     vectors (MARTHA/MARHTA=0.9611, DWAYNE/DUANE=0.8400,
     DIXON/DICKSONX=0.8133) + token-set/overlap scoring for reordered
     names. Explicit tier thresholds (high >=0.92, low >=0.80); anything
     below 0.80 on every signal is NOT surfaced as a match candidate at
     all -- a documented false-negative risk, not a hidden implementation
     detail.
   - watchlist.adapters.xml -- a portable zero-dependency XML-string reader,
     the same hand-rolled pattern kotoba-lang/org-omg-bpmn's bpmn.xml,
     org-oasis-open-xmile's xmile.xml, org-omg-uml's uml.xml, and
     org-sbml's sbml.xml each already carry independently (house
     convention per kotoba-lang/composer's ADR-2607031510: this utility is
     small enough that duplicating it per-consumer beats a shared
     dependency).
   - watchlist.adapters.ofac-sdn / .un-consolidated -- real parsers for
     each source's actual schema (verified against live data, see
     Context). watchlist.adapters.eu-consolidated -- documented
     not-implemented stub, throws with a clear reason rather than silently
     no-op'ing.
   - watchlist.ports/IWatchlistIndex + watchlist.adapters.edn-index --
     durable file-backed index (JVM-only .clj, mirroring
     ekyc.adapters.edn-provider's own precedent for the same file-I/O
     reason), reading <source>.entities.edn + <source>.manifest.edn per
     source.
   - watchlist.core/screen -- orchestration that ALWAYS returns staleness
     alongside candidates (:watchlist/stale?, :watchlist/list-age-days) --
     an index that never loaded at all fails closed, exactly like every
     loaded source being past its staleness threshold, never looking like
     a clean check.
   - watchlist.adapters.aml-port -- a real aml.ports/IAmlScreening
     implementation, mirroring aml.adapters.etzhayyim/screening-port's
     own documented fail-closed discipline ('non-adjudicating' always,
     never :clear on an absent/unrecognized signal): an absent screenable
     name, a stale/absent index, and zero candidates are three different
     states that never collapse to the same :clear. NOT yet wired into
     aml.core/screen's actual routing -- aml.model/routes is
     [:yabai :malak], a closed vector this repo does not own; adding
     :watchlist requires a small separate follow-up PR to kotoba-lang/aml.
   - scripts/refresh_lists.cljs (nbb, per this workspace's script-host
     rule -- no bb/babashka) -- real fetch/hash(sha256)/parse/write,
     run against both live sources during this session (see the shipped
     manifest.edn files' sha256/entity-count fields, and this repo's
     initial commit shipping a real 4.9MB snapshot: 19,169 OFAC + 1,010 UN
     entities, not synthetic data).

   38 tests / 93 assertions, 0 failures (including fixture parsing tests
   whose fixtures are schema-faithful but use fabricated names, never real
   designated-person data verbatim). clj-kondo: 0 errors, 0 warnings."
  :adr/consequences
  ["Any AML consumer in this workspace (the planned cloud-itonami-isic-649 verification actor, or any future consumer) gets real sanctions/PEP screening against actual current government data instead of the aml repo's own documented gap ('does not fetch live lists')."
   "watchlist.adapters.eu-consolidated's explicit not-implemented stub means a caller wiring all three sources gets an immediate, honest failure instead of a silent no-op or a fabricated EU schema this repo never actually verified against live data."
   "Adding a real :watchlist route to aml.core/screen's routing remains a separate, small, explicitly-tracked follow-up PR to kotoba-lang/aml -- not bundled into this ADR, since this repo does not own that file."
   "resources/watchlist/lists/ ships a point-in-time real snapshot that will silently age unless scripts/refresh_lists.cljs is re-run or scheduled -- no CI cron wired yet (MATURITY.md's own follow-up list)."
   "The shared watchlist.adapters.xml parser is a straightforward char-scanning implementation, not optimized for the ~28MB OFAC file (tens of seconds on the JVM) -- acceptable for a periodic batch refresh, not a hot path."]
  :adr/references [2607198200 2607031510]}]
